Used as \code{values} of a scale, the first \code{n} patterns are taken. A \code{"-"} prefix reverses the order.
With \code{tm_patterns()}, a set is expanded to all its patterns, which can be subsetted, e.g.
\code{tm_patterns("classic")[c(1, 3, 5)]}.

The patterns are shown with \code{\link[=tmap_show_values]{tmap_show_values()}}: all types and shortcuts with
\code{tmap_show_values("pattern")}, and a set with e.g. \code{tmap_show_values("pattern", values = "classic")}.

The spacing between the pattern elements is determined by the map variable
\code{pattern_density}, the line width or dot size by \code{pattern_size}, and the color by \code{pattern_col}.
The parameters \code{length} and \code{amplitude} are relative to that spacing, so the whole
motif is scaled with the density.

The pattern type \code{"terrain"} is a texture rather than a motif: random relief, shaded with light
from the northwest, which looks like rough terrain. It is drawn as highlights (white) and shadows
(\code{pattern_col}) on top of the fill color, which cancel out roughly, so the average color does not
change much. Its strength is determined by \code{pattern_density} (between 0, no texture, and 1), which
makes it suitable for showing uncertainty: the rougher, the less certain. \code{pattern_size} is not used.

Two other textures are meant to show uncertainty about the fill color rather than about heights:
\itemize{
\item \code{"mottle"} looks like a surface that is painted poorly: large patches with sharp edges, painted in a
few (\code{levels}) shades that are slightly darker (\code{pattern_col}) or brighter (white) than the intended
color. As for \code{"terrain"}, \code{pattern_density} is the strength, and \code{contrast} scales the differences between
the shades. By default, the shades follow smooth noise, so a patch only borders on patches of the next
darker or brighter shade, which may look like a density surface or elevation. With \code{random = 1}, each
patch gets a random shade, so that any shades can be neighbors.
\item \code{"sketch"} looks like a sketchy pencil drawing: the fill color is only applied in pencil strokes,
with white paper in between. \code{pattern_density} determines how much of the paper is visible, from
0 (fully colored) to 1 (about two thirds paper). \code{pattern_col} and \code{pattern_size} are not used.
Since the paper is drawn on top of the fill, it is always white, regardless of the background.
}

In plot mode, these textures require a graphics device that supports clipping paths.
